example.paragraph:
Compute $\mathbf{v}_{2} - \mathbf{v}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\
        4 \\
        {- .5}
        \end{bmatrix}$ and $\textbf{v}_{3} - \textbf{v}_{1} = ~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        {- 1} \\
        1 \\
        0
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$ These two points are not multiples and hence form a linearly independent set, $S\prime$. So all statements in Theorem 5 are false, and $S$ is affinely independent. Figure 2 shows $S$ and the translated set $S\prime$. Notice that $\text{Span~}S\prime$ is a plane through the origin and $\text{aff~}S$ is a parallel plane through $\textbf{v}_{1}$, $\textbf{v}_{2}$, and $\textbf{v}_{3}$. (Only a portion of each plane is shown here, of course.)

