content.paragraph:
If $f$ is a linear functional on $\text{ℝ}^{n}$, then the standard matrix of this linear transformation $f$ is a $1 \times n$ matrix $A$, say $A = \begin{bmatrix}
        a_{1} & a_{2} & \cdots & a_{n}
        \end{bmatrix}$. So

example.paragraph:
Let $\textbf{n} = \begin{bmatrix}
        3 \\
        4
        \end{bmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        1 \\
        {- 6}
        \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and let $H = \left\{ \textbf{x}:\textbf{n} \cdot \textbf{x} = 12 \right\}$, so $H = \left\lbrack f\text{:}12 \right\rbrack$, where $f(x,y) = 3x + 4y$. Thus $H$ is the line $3x + 4y = 12$. Find an implicit description of the parallel hyperplane (line) $H_{1} = H + \textbf{v.}$﻿

First, find a point $\textbf{p}$ in $H_{1}$. To do this, find a point in $H$ and add $\textbf{v}$ to it. For instance, $\begin{bmatrix}
        0 \\
        3
        \end{bmatrix}$ is in $H,$ so $\textbf{p} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        1 \\
        {- 6}
        \end{array} \right\rbrack + \begin{bmatrix}
        0 \\
        3
        \end{bmatrix} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        1 \\
        {- 3}
        \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is in $H_{1}$. Now, compute $\textbf{n} \cdot \textbf{p} = - 9$. This shows that $H_{1} = \left\lbrack f\text{:} - 9 \right\rbrack$. See Figure 2, which also shows the subspace $H_{0} = \left\{ \textbf{x}:\textbf{n} \cdot \textbf{x} = 0 \right\}$.﻿

This amounts to solving a nonhomogeneous equation $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$, where $A = \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & {- 4}
        \end{bmatrix}$ and $\textbf{b}$ is the number $13$ in $\text{ℝ}$. Write $x = 13 + 4y$, where $y$ is a free variable. In parametric form, the solution is

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{lllll}
        {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
        a & b
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
        x \\
        y
        \end{array} \right\rbrack = 0} & \text{or} & {\mathbf{n} \cdot \mathbf{x} = 0,} & \text{where} & {\mathbf{n} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
        a \\
        b
        \end{array} \right\rbrack}
        \end{array}$$

example.paragraph:
By inspection, a solution is ${\text{[}\begin{matrix}
        a & b
        \end{matrix}\text{]}} = {\text{[}\begin{matrix}
        2 & 5
        \end{matrix}\text{]}}$. Let $f(x,y) = 2x + 5y$. From (5), $L_{0} = \lbrack f:0\rbrack$, and $L_{1} = \lbrack f:d\rbrack$ for some $d$. Since $\textbf{v}_{1}$ is on line $L_{1}$, $d = f\left( \textbf{v}_{1} \right) = 2(1) + 5(2) = 12$. Thus, the equation for $L_{1}$ is $2x + 5y = 12$. As a check, note that $f\left( \textbf{v}_{2} \right) = f(6,0) = 2(6) + 5(0) = 12$, so $\textbf{v}_{2}$ is on $L_{1}$, too.

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{lll}
        {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{lrl}
        1 & {- 2} & 3
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
        a \\
        b \\
        c
        \end{array} \right\rbrack = 0,} & {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
        2 & 0 & 1
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
        a \\
        b \\
        c
        \end{array} \right\rbrack = 0,} & \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lrll}
        1 & {- 2} & 3 & 0 \\
        2 & 0 & 1 & 0
        \end{array} \right\rbrack
        \end{array}$$

statement.paragraph:
Let $L$ be the line in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ through the points $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        1 \\
        4
        \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\begin{bmatrix}
        {- 2} \\
        {- 1}
        \end{bmatrix}$. Find a linear functional $f$ and a real number $d$ such that $L = \lbrack f:d\rbrack$.﻿

Let $\textbf{a}_{1} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\
        {- 1} \\
        5
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, $\textbf{a}_{2} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        3 \\
        1 \\
        3
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, $\textbf{a}_{3} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        {- 1} \\
        6 \\
        0
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, $\textbf{b}_{1} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        0 \\
        5 \\
        {- 1}
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, $\textbf{b}_{2} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\
        {- 3} \\
        {- 2}
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, $\textbf{b}_{3} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\
        2 \\
        1
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, and $\textbf{n} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        3 \\
        1 \\
        {- 2}
        \end{bmatrix}\,$, and let $A = \{\textbf{a}_{1},\textbf{a}_{2},\textbf{a}_{3}\}$ and $B = \left\{ \textbf{b}_{1},\textbf{b}_{2},\textbf{b}_{3} \right\}$. Find a hyperplane $H$ with normal $\textbf{n}$ that separates $A$ and $B$. Is there a hyperplane parallel to $H$ that strictly separates $A$ and $B$?

Let $A$ be the $1 \times 4$ matrix $\lbrack\begin{matrix}
        1 & {- 3} & 4 & {- 2}
        \end{matrix}\rbrack$ and let $b = 5$. Let $H = \text{\{}\textbf{x}\text{~in~}\text{ℝ}^{4}:A\textbf{x} = b\text{\}}$.﻿

Let $A$ be the $1 \times 5$ matrix $\text{[}\begin{matrix}
        2 & 5 & {- 3} & 0 & 6
        \end{matrix}\text{]}$. Note that $\text{Nul~}A$ is in $\text{ℝ}^{5}$. Let $H = \text{Nul~}A$.﻿

Let $H$ be the column space of the matrix $B = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 0 \\
        4 & 2 \\
        {- 7} & {- 6}
        \end{bmatrix}\,$. That is, $H = \text{Col~}B$. [Hint: How is Col $B$ related to $\text{Nul~}B^{T}$? See Section 6.1.]

Let $H$ be the column space of the matrix $B = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 0 \\
        5 & 2 \\
        {- 4} & {- 4}
        \end{bmatrix}\,$. That is, $H = \text{Col~}B$.﻿

Let $\textbf{v}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\
        1
        \end{bmatrix}$, $\textbf{v}_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}
        3 \\
        0
        \end{bmatrix}$, $\textbf{v}_{3} = \begin{bmatrix}
        5 \\
        3
        \end{bmatrix}$, and $\textbf{p} = \begin{bmatrix}
        4 \\
        1
        \end{bmatrix}$. Find a hyperplane $\lbrack f:d\rbrack$ (in this case, a line) that strictly separates $\textbf{p}$ from $\text{conv}\left\{ \textbf{v}_{1},\textbf{v}_{2},\textbf{v}_{3} \right\}$.﻿

Repeat Exercise 29 for $\textbf{v}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\
        2
        \end{bmatrix}$, $\textbf{v}_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}
        5 \\
        1
        \end{bmatrix}$, $\textbf{v}_{3} = \begin{bmatrix}
        4 \\
        4
        \end{bmatrix}$, and $\textbf{p} = \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\
        3
        \end{bmatrix}$.﻿

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{lll}
        m & = & {f\left( \mathbf{v}\prime\text{)} = f\left( c_{1}\mathbf{v}_{1} + \cdots + c_{k}\mathbf{v}_{k} \right) \right.} \\
         & = & {c_{1}f\left( \mathbf{v}_{1} \right) + \cdots + c_{k}f\left( \mathbf{v}_{k} \right)} \\
         & < & {c_{1}m + \cdots + c_{k}m = m\left( c_{1} + \cdots + c_{k} \right) = m}
        \end{array}$$

example.paragraph:
Given points $\textbf{p}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        {- 1} \\
        0
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\text{,}$ $\textbf{p}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        3 \\
        1
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\text{,}$ and $\textbf{p}_{3} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
        1 \\
        2
        \end{array} \right\rbrack$ in ${\mathbb{R}}^{2}\text{,}$ let $S = \text{conv}\{\textbf{p}_{1},\textbf{p}_{2},\textbf{p}_{3}\}\text{.}$ For each linear functional $f\text{,}$ find the maximum value $m$ of $f$ on the set $S\text{,}$ and find all points $\textbf{x}$ in $S$ at which $f(\textbf{x}) = m\text{.}$

